.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt LOGS 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm logs
.Nd inspect bounded boot and service history
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -volatile
.Op Fl -list-boots | Fl -sources | Fl -health
.Op Fl b Ar 0 | -1 | all | boot
.Op Fl u Ar source
.Op Fl p Ar 0..7
.Op Fl -since Ar ticks
.Op Fl -after Ar sequence
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl -export | Fl -raw-file Ar path
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the retained journal under
.Pa /var/log/journal .
By default it shows the newest 50 records in the newest retained boot.
.Fl b Cm -1
selects the previous available boot;
.Fl b Cm all
selects both retained boots.
An unavailable previous boot is reported as
.Dq no previous boot retained
with nonzero status; an explicit identity absent from retention is also an error.
A known selected boot with no records matching the other filters succeeds
with empty output. Boot aliases resolve before filtering.
Boot identities are the kernel's 32 lowercase hexadecimal digits.
.Fl -list-boots
lists available boot identities, oldest first; an explicit
.Fl b
limits the listed identities to that selection.
.Fl -sources
lists retained sources for the selected boot and filters.
.Fl -health
shows the last published collector PID, monotonic sample tick and persistence counters.
Current collector liveness, persistence, error and consecutive-unproductive
count are explicitly unknown. Each field from the old file is prefixed
.Li sample_ .
A read-only current-boot kernel-ring scan may display the latest retained
supervisor count, boot, tick and sequence. This is an observed count, not
live authority; it may be overwritten or unavailable. A recorded PID is
shown as a number and a recorded lack of error as
.Li none ;
other error detail is preserved and escaped.
The stored health file and J1 export are unchanged.
The human values use
.Li sample_collector_pid=4 ,
.Li sample_collector_pid=unknown ,
and
.Li sample_error=none .
Every recorded error value is preserved as a whole under
.Li sample_error ,
including optional-value wrappers; only the exact recorded
.Li None
becomes
.Li none .
Unknown or malformed values remain escaped as recorded.
Unrecognized sample text is escaped verbatim, including its field names.
This is a retained observation, not proof the collector is alive now.
If a boot selector is supplied, the selected identity must be retained and
match the snapshot's own boot; an unavailable matching health snapshot is
an error rather than a fallback to another boot.
A protocol refusal of the optional default health sample is shown as
.Dq Retained sample unavailable
with an explicit reason, while current health remains unknown and the
independent kernel observation is still attempted. It does not mean that a
successfully acquired sample was empty. Actual health-file read errors
are reported with nonzero status. Neither refusal nor read failure can
satisfy an explicit boot selection. The independent observation is scoped
to the current boot; it cannot supply a supervisor observation for a
selected historical boot.
.Pp
.Fl u
matches an exact trusted source or a service name such as
.Cm sshd
or
.Cm fsd .
.Fl p
includes priorities from zero through the given number (zero is most severe).
Stdout is priority 6, stderr priority 4, and service lifecycle priority 5.
Known routine SSH authentication, exec-start, successful exit and reclamation
records use stdout. Nonzero or abnormal exits, refusals and unclassified
failures remain stderr; a command exit status alone is not a service-health
verdict.
.Fl -since
selects a monotonic tick lower bound within each selected boot.
.Fl -after
selects sequences greater than the given value within each boot.
When producer time is unknown, flag 8589934592 marks collector acquisition
time (the volatile view uses zero for unknown producer ticks).
Ticks are not wall-clock seconds and sequences do not establish a perfect
order between independent kernel and service producers.
.Fl f
polls every 250 ticks, prints each newly observed retained sequence once,
and reports retention gaps when no source/priority filter hides records.
A blocked reader does not own or stall collection.
.Fl -export
prints all selected retained records as ordinary escaped text, suitable for
redirection, grep, sed and tail.
.Fl n
limits the initial recent display, up to 10000 records.
.Pp
.Fl -volatile
acquires data from the independent kernel/service ring without consulting
persistent history, networking,
or persistent storage. It includes current-boot service output captured by
init. It is current-boot only: default,
.Fl b Cm 0 ,
.Fl b Cm all
and the explicit current identity select only this boot.
Previous or foreign identities fail before output, including with
.Fl -health ,
.Fl -sources
or
.Fl -list-boots .
This route has finite ring retention and reports overwritten records.
The recent-display cap applies only to the initial window and prints an
explicit truncation notice. Follow streams subsequent records directly;
export streams the complete initially retained window, reporting cursor gaps.
Use
.Xr dmesg 1
for kernel-oriented inspection.
.Pp
Records use the line-oriented J1 format:
.Dl J1 boot sequence ticks priority source kernel-sequence flags escaped-text
Source and service/PID/stream metadata come from the kernel and init;
application text cannot choose them.
Control bytes, non-ASCII bytes and backslashes are written as
.Li \exHH .
The terminal never interprets application escape sequences.
.Fl -raw-file
creates a new named file containing decoded retained message bytes, including
trusted message prefixes and any truncation/suppression notices.
It refuses to replace an existing file and never emits decoded bytes to the
terminal. Treat that explicit export as arbitrary application bytes.
.Sh RETENTION AND FAILURE
Init supervises a separate non-spawning
.Pa absd-logd
collector with a read-only Diagnostics grant and one exclusive private FSD
channel. Its bounded filesystem requests never share the SSH control channel.
FSD retains its own local diagnostics and continues serving during collector
death and backoff.
Init drains separate managed-service stdout/stderr pipes into the kernel
ring independently of FSD. Ordinary applications log through stdout/stderr.
Private console input and credential material are not collector inputs.
.Pp
The journal keeps two files for the current boot and two for one previous
boot, at most 128 KiB per file (512 KiB total payload).
The existing fsd and sshd text projections each keep their existing two
64 KiB files. Collector health is a separate small fixed file.
Each journal message retains at most 320 producer bytes, with explicit
truncation. The collector keeps at most 64 KiB of recent encoded records
in memory, at most one quarter per source, and at most 16 distinct sources.
Six of those entries reserve admission for
.Ql kernel ,
.Ql unknown ,
.Ql collector ,
.Ql service.sshd ,
.Ql service.fsd ,
and
.Ql service.logd ;
other typed labels share the remaining ten entries.
A reserved name appears in the source listing only after a record is retained.
Unambiguous inferred kernel first-word hints are grouped under
.Ql kernel ;
the original hint remains in producer text and the INFERRED flag is retained.
Damaged LOSS records retain unknown attribution.
The independent dmesg view still shows the original kernel hint.
.Pp
Each source admits 32 records per 1000 ticks; later records in that window
are suppressed and counted. The next admitted record reports that source's
suppression, and health includes total loss, rotation and eviction counts.
.Pp
A failed append retains ordered pending work in the existing bounded memory
queue and backs off for 1000 current collector ticks. Idle polls retry too.
Unacknowledged memory evictions count as unconfirmed sink loss; retry attempts
do not. The independent volatile route remains available. Full, read-only and unavailable sinks are not
reported as durable. A damaged FSD mount follows the existing service
failure and maintenance policy. The collector performs at most eight ring
reads per pass; ordinary filesystem requests have a 100-tick total transport bound;
COMMIT and the logger handshake allow 1000 ticks within the caller's remaining
deadline. A complete
logd pass has a 4000-tick deadline below the 5000-tick liveness threshold. There is no reader-owned collection queue.
.Pp
Init reaps the exact collector process before handing its private channel to
a successor. It waits 1000 ticks and permits at most four successful crash replacements
per boot, then reports exhaustion through the independent volatile route.
Unproductive attempts back off for 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 30, then 60 seconds, and
continue retrying at the 60-second cap when storage remains unavailable.
The count saturates. The first complete notice of a newly acquired and synced
kernel record starts a productivity interval; only a later new-record sync
at least 60 seconds afterward in the same child resets the count.
Collection notices occur at most once per minute after the first.
READY, heartbeats, lifetime alone and reopening old history are insufficient.
After initial transitions, retry summaries appear every 60th unproductive
attempt. Non-75 outcomes and bounded child output remain visible.
Later exhaustion, quarantine and unconfirmed-child observations replace older
retry or collection observations; unavailable counts remain unknown. The logger's pre-main VERSION check has a 1000-tick bound;
only its transport timeout requests transient reattachment. Other clients
retain the existing startup bound. The logs command defers negotiation until
filesystem use, with the same mandatory protocol check at the common request
boundary; changing the startup name cannot bypass it.
.Fl Fl volatile
sends no FSD request.
.Fl Fl health
uses a checked 1000-tick VERSION probe for the optional retained sample.
This bounds VERSION only; subsequent ordinary sample I/O may still wait if
the server stalls after answering VERSION. Use
.Fl Fl volatile
for no FSD request.
Protocol refusal reports that sample unavailable and still permits unknown
current health and independent kernel supervisor observations. Status 0 for
this partial diagnostic report does not assert healthy collection or
persistence. Actual sample file I/O errors remain visible and return status 1;
ordinary retained-query protocol refusal exits 104 before filesystem use. Unknown child reaping authority stops
replacement and further raw-handle actions, while unrelated children may
still be reaped; ambiguous outcomes are reported as unclassified.
Stale replies and partial frames are discarded at verified quiet boundaries;
the private logger handshake retires all previous-generation open handles.
A refused construction creates no process and consumes no crash restart.
Unknown OPEN, CLOSE or handshake completion, or exhausted logger handles,
fences further storage operations and ends logd with transient status 75.
Init reaps that exact child before reconnecting after backoff; these attachment
retries neither add to nor refund the crash replacement count.
Orderly drain makes one immediate retry of pending backoff and stops on no
progress, within its 1000-tick absolute transport deadline.
No application can obtain that handshake or a writable journal mount.
Collector restart resumes after the last persisted kernel sequence for this
boot and declares that unpersisted records may replay. Kernel sequence and
boot identify such duplicates. Kernel cursor gaps and incomplete trailing
writes are reported. A partial final record is never displayed as complete.
Abrupt loss during boot-file rollover may leave only part of the previous
boot; each surviving record still carries its own boot identity.
.Sh DURABILITY
Buffered means available only in the volatile ring or memory. Written means
accepted by the filesystem; flushed means covered by a successful device
sync. Health reports written and flushed sequence high-water marks; loss
counters cover only the named collector generation and reset on restart.
A high-water mark does not fill gaps. Initial acquisition reports ring records
already overwritten before the collector started. A timeout leaves completion
unconfirmed: reopening reconciles complete journal records before retrying.
The retained health file is a sample, not collector liveness;
.Fl Fl health
labels its persistence state at the sample and reports liveness unknown.
After startup, changed journal data is synced at most once per 1000 ticks.
Idle collection does not sync or rewrite health merely to advance time.
Changed health counters produce a separately flushed receipt.
Orderly shutdown closes the collector control pipe, drains at most 128 ring
records within a 1000-tick absolute filesystem deadline,
then attempts one sync and reports flushed, partial, unconfirmed or failed.
A transport timeout cannot establish a failed commit.
Block operations retain init's existing finite FSD stop/liveness bounds;
shutdown does not add an unbounded logging retry loop.
FSD serves the collector for at most 2000 ticks after SSH control EOF; neither
waits without a bound for the other. Init reaps the collector before waiting
for FSD and before granting the guarded finalizer another bounded drain.
The finalizer observes service outcomes before recording orderly completion.
.Pp
An installed writable profile retains selected current/previous-boot records
through ordinary reboot after successful flushing. This does not promise
survival of final panic output, abrupt power loss, or failing media.
Logs are diagnostics, not a tamper-proof authorization ledger.
.Sh AUTHORITY
Authenticated operator sessions may read logs only when granted the
.Pa /var/log
read-only mount. A session cannot acquire a writable mount overlapping this
tree, create forged journal records, or remove retained history.
The dedicated collector writes the journal through FSD; the privileged SSH
control channel retains its
legacy log-write authority but sees the journal and health as read-only.
Destructive retention is not exposed by
.Nm .
Deferred protocol negotiation grants no extra filesystem authority.
The common runtime request boundary still requires checked protocol admission.
Once loaded, the volatile view sends no FSD request; executable loading may
itself depend on FSD. Availability of loading a new command during an FSD
outage is not established by this behavior.
.Pp
The volatile reader requires an already-owned read-only Diagnostics capability,
separate from console input, logging write and machine-control authority.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a successful query, including a valid selection with zero
filter matches, or a default health response explicitly reporting optional
sample refusal and unknown current state. An acquired-input error remains a
failure even if the display pipe also closes. A consumer closing an output
pipe otherwise ends quietly with
zero status; this does not assert that the entire history was delivered.
This applies to every display, including volatile collector health.
Acquisition errors and explicit file-export errors remain failures, even
when their error kind is broken pipe.
Unavailable history or other acquisition/output errors return one;
invalid arguments return two.
.Fl h
and
.Fl -help
print usage to standard output and return zero without acquiring history.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl logs --list-boots
.Dl logs -u sshd -p 4 -n 20
.Dl logs -b -1 --export
.Dl logs -u fsd -f
.Dl logs --volatile --health
.Dl logs --volatile -u sshd -f
.Dl logs --export > /home/operator/history.txt
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dmesg 1 ,
.Xr health 1 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
.Xr reboot 8
